<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Project xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/developer/msbuild/2003">
  <!--
    Which toolchain builds this tree (ADR-018 A24, review finding CPP-4).

    Separate from Outpost.Compile.props because MSBuild gives the two settings
    two different homes, and putting either in the other's place fails quietly.
    PlatformToolset has to be defined before Microsoft.Cpp.props consumes it, so
    this file is imported ahead of Microsoft.Cpp.Default.props; the compiler
    switches have to survive the toolset's own defaults, so those live in a
    property sheet imported after Microsoft.Cpp.props. A toolset set too late
    does not error - it silently falls back to whatever the installed VS
    defaults to, which on the runner is the same number and so would look like
    it worked.

    Nine projects each carried their own copy of this line, twice (once per
    configuration). One file is the point: the pin is a decision, and a decision
    stated in nine places is a decision nobody can change.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Label="OutpostToolchain">
    <!--
      v145 (VS 2026). Pinned here and deliberately *not* pinned in the CI
      workflow: A2 records why. The runner image is whatever GitHub ships, and a
      wrong image pin trades a floating toolchain for a broken build, so the
      workflow prints image, VS, MSBuild and dxc versions every run and the pin
      can be taken from evidence when it is wanted.
    -->
    <PlatformToolset>v145</PlatformToolset>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
